te at a  reason weekend event but Andrew Colson  who is an education director there he  has done a lot of work to kind of  quantify how we've been doing and what  you see the blue line is the total cost  of education the first one going way up  in 1970 adjusted for inflation it cost  about fifty eight thousand dollars to  send a kid for their lifetime of there K  through 12 education every year and in  2010 it cost about 165,000 dollars for a  lifetime of K through 12 education the  red line is how much staffing has gone  up way more people work in the schools  the next one that's going up the dotted  line is how much enrollment is gone up  since 1970 so you can see the red line  with staffing has gone up a lot more  than the enrollment line so we have wave  more adults that we're paying to work  with a relatively flat number of kids  and then the green and the red and the  purplish line that's all student  achievement for 17 year olds on the  National Assessment of Education  progress so we can see that we used to  an inflation-adjusted dollars spend a  lot less to get the exact same outcomes  for 17 year olds in terms of reading  math and science similarly this next  slide is just federal spending and the  federal government used to give K  through 12 a lot less money than they do  now and again federal spending has shot  through the ceiling and all of those  academic performance on the nape for 17  year olds are still flat in reading math  in science so we've spent a lot of money  we haven't seen a lot of improvement and  that's basically why we want more  competition besides the fact that  obviously want parents to have choice  and control their kids destinies and we  want schools to look more like pop-tarts  and less like  the one plain sad pop tart with no  frosting on it here we have how much we  spend in the United States on k-12  education compared to the rest of the  world and really where we go along with  Switzerland as the top spending of all  developed