d at the same  time help tell the stories of parents  and kids and teachers and individual  schools who are truly seeing their lives  and and communities changed as a result  of freedom and education and I think  that if we look at the growth of school  choice in America today and also the  demand for school choice in individual  communities across the country we have  the potential to see right now in the  next five to ten years an exponential  growth in the amount of school choice  and I think one of the reasons we're  going to see that is number one we've  reached a tipping point in terms of how  many people are benefitting from school  choice parents are seeing other parents  actually being able to choose the right  schools for their kids as opposed to  being stuck in some arbitrary zip code  you know mandated schools that's number  one number two so many so-called reforms  recently whether it's testing as Lisa  talked about with new standards have  really not been popular with parents the  new standards program has not been  implemented as effectively as it could  have been and people frankly are really  mad they want more options and school  choice in my view and in the view I hope  of many of you here is the single  catalytic reform it's the reform giving  parents freedom and opportunity to  reform that can get all other reforms  done because it is the reform that  parents understand they understand that  if they choose the right school for  their child great things will happen  what makes a lot more sense to  individual parents in local communities  than teacher quality reform standard  reforms test  reforms spending reforms things like  that choice is truly the great equalizer  so with that I want to talk about why  national school choice week exists and  how we play a role in this overall  movement because as much as I'd love to  take credit for the growth in school  choice it has something to do it with me  really it has to do with individual  parents and